From 6721db3b0b421cea8843534d4cf1c3df78eb1ac1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Florian Stosse <1315-Harvester57@users.noreply.git.frama-c.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 21:38:14 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [libc] strlcat: result from the pre-state, no write past n
 bytes

The contract of strlcat had two wrong clauses:

- "ensures bounded_result: \result == strlen(dest) + strlen(src)" read
  the length of dest after the call. strlcat returns the length of the
  string it tried to create: the length of dest before the call, or n
  when dest has no NUL within its first n bytes, plus the length of
  src. With dest "abcdef", src "x" and n 3, Eva gave 7 where the
  function returns 4, and WP proved the post-state equation for a
  caller that appends "cd" to "ab" (the result is 4, the sum is 6).
- "assigns dest[strlen(dest)..n]" included dest[n], one byte past the
  n bytes the function may write, and every byte up to it. A field
  that follows the buffer was considered modified.

State the result with \old and the minimum with n, and stop the frame
at the last byte the function writes. Also name the bytes of dest and
all the bytes of src among the dependencies of the result.

New test: tests/libc/strlcat.c. tests/libc/string_h.c: a check that was
unknown ("imprecise") is now valid.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
